.TH CRASH 8V "July 11, 1987"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
crash \- what happens when the system crashes
.SH DESCRIPTION
This section explains what happens when the system crashes
and (very briefly) how to analyze crash dumps.
.PP
When the system crashes voluntarily it prints a message of the form
.IP
panic: why i gave up the ghost
.LP
on the console, takes a dump in the swap area,
and then invokes an automatic reboot procedure as
described in
.IR reboot (8).
Unless some unexpected inconsistency is encountered in the state
of the file systems due to hardware or software failure, the system
will then resume multi-user operations.
If the automatic file system
check fails, the file systems should be checked and repaired with
.IR fsck (8)
before continuing.
.PP
The system has a large number of internal consistency checks; if one
of these fails, then it will panic with a very short message indicating
which one failed.
In many instances, this will be the name of the routine which detected
the error, or a two-word description of the inconsistency.
A full understanding of most panic messages requires perusal of the
source code for the system.
.PP
The most common cause of system failures is hardware failure, which
can reflect itself in different ways.  Here are the messages which
are most likely, with some hints as to causes.
Left unstated in all cases is the possibility that hardware or software
error produced the message in some unexpected way.
.TP
.B iinit
This cryptic panic message results from a failure to mount the root filesystem
during the bootstrap process.
Either the root filesystem has been corrupted,
or the system is attempting to use the wrong device as root filesystem.
Usually, an alternate copy of the system binary or an alternate root
filesystem can be used to bring up the system to investigate.
.TP
.B swsiz
.ns
.TP
.B swtyp
The swap area, partition b of the disk the system was started from,
is not there, or its type in the disk's label is not swap
(see
.IR disklabel (8));
the system will not swap over what may be a file system.
.TP
.B hard err: swap
The system encountered an error trying to write to the swap device
or an error in reading critical information from a disk drive.
The offending disk should be fixed if it is broken or unreliable.
.TP
.B timeout table overflow
.ns
This really shouldn't be a panic, but until the data structure
involved is made to be extensible, running out of entries causes a crash.
If this happens, make the timeout table bigger.
(NCALL in param.c)
.TP
.B trap
An unexpected trap has occurred within the system, and it has printed
.RS
.IP
trap type \fIt\fP err \fIe\fP in the kernel
.PP
and the registers, as they were when it occurred, on two lines.
The trap types are the processor's exceptions, in decimal:
.PP
.nf
0	divide error
1	single step
3	breakpoint
4	overflow (INTO)
5	BOUND
6	invalid instruction
8	double fault
10	invalid task state segment
11	segment not present
12	stack segment
13	general protection
16	287 error
.fi
.PP
The error code is what the processor gave with the exception: for 10
to 13 the selector it could not use, or 0.
Then the code segment and instruction pointer,
.IR cs : ip ,
the flags and the data segment registers, and the other registers.
.PP
The favorite trap type in system crashes is 13, general protection,
indicating a wild reference: an offset past the end of a segment, or
a selector that is not one.  These problems tend to be easy to track
down if they are kernel bugs since the processor stops cold, but random
flakiness seems to cause this sometimes.
The debugger can be used to locate the instruction and subroutine
corresponding to
.IR cs : ip ,
which it takes as one number, the selector in its upper 16 bits.
If that is insufficient to suggest the nature of the problem,
more detailed examination of the system status at the time of the trap
usually can produce an explanation.
.RE
.TP
.B init died
The system initialization process has exited, or could not be started:
.I /etc/init
is not there, or its mode or type forbids execution.
This is bad news, as no new
users will then be able to log in.  Rebooting is the only fix, so the
system just does it right away.
.TP
out of swap space
This really shouldn't be panics but there's no other
satisfactory solution.
The size of the swap area must be increased.
The system attempts to avoid running out of swap by refusing to
start new processes when short of swap space (resulting in
``No more proceses'' messages from the shell).
.PP
That completes the list of panic types you are likely to see.
There are many other panic messages which are less likely to occur;
most of them detect logical inconsistencies within the kernel
and thus ``cannot happen'' unless some part of the kernel has been modified.
.PP
A PC has no console switches with which to force a panic: if the
system stops or hangs without one, no dump can be taken, and the
machine is started again with its reset button or its power switch.
.PP
After rebooting, or after an automatic file system check fails,
check and fix the file systems with
.IR fsck .
If the system will not reboot, a runnable system must be obtained
from a backup medium after verifying that the hardware is functioning normally.
A damaged root file system should be patched while running with an alternate
root if possible.
.PP
When the system crashes it writes (or at least attempts to write)
an image of memory into the swap area of the disk it was started
from, 512 sectors in, so that what is swapped out as it starts again
does not write over it.
The image is the memory from address 0 to its end, the hole from 640K
to 1M with it, and as much of that as the swap area has room for;
the system's own tables are in the first 640K.
The disk's driver writes it, which
.I wd
and
.I sd
do: a system started from the RAM disk of the diskette it is
installed from takes no dump.
.I "reboot \-d"
takes one too, without a panic.
After the system is rebooted, the program
.IR savecore (8)
runs and preserves a copy of this core image and the current
system in a specified directory for later perusal, if
.I /etc/crash
is that directory or a symbolic link to it.  See
.IR savecore (8)
for details.
.PP
To analyze a dump you should begin by running
.IR adb (1)
with the
.B \-k
flag on the system load image and core dump that
.I savecore
saved, such as
.I "adb \-k /usr/crash/unix.0 /usr/crash/core.0" .
The command
``*_panicstr/s''
prints the panic message, if the core image is the result of a panic,
and
``$c'' or ``$C''
will provide a stack trace from the point of
the crash, back through
.I panic
to what called it, and this will provide a clue as to
what went wrong.
The scripts in
.I /usr/share/adb
print the system's structures:
``*_uptr$<u''
the user structure of the process that was running, and
``_proc$<proc''
the first entry of the process table.
.IR ps (1)
and
.IR pstat (8)
can also be used
to print the process table at the time of the crash via:
.I "ps \-alx \-k /usr/crash/core.0:/usr/crash/unix.0"
and
.IR "pstat \-p \-k /usr/crash/unix.0:/usr/crash/core.0" .
.PP
The image begins with a header that says where the global descriptor
table is in it, whose slots for the system's segments say where each
of them is, and where the frame of the routine that took the dump was,
from which $c starts
.RI ( <machine/seg.h> ).
The selector of each segment, and an address in it, are as in
.IR /dev/kmem
(see
.IR adb (1)).
The stack of the process that was running is in its slot of the
system's data segment, and at
``_tsleep'' or ``_swtch''
the stack of another process whose user structure is in a slot shows
where it is waiting.
.PP
A more complete discussion
of system debugging is impossible here.
See, however,
``Using ADB to Debug the UNIX Kernel''.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
adb(1), ps(1), pstat(8), boot(8), disklabel(8), fsck(8), reboot(8),
savecore(8)
.br
.I "Using ADB to Debug the UNIX Kernel"
